  "live_records": {
    "q-15074": {
      "dom_id": "step-q15074",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14274523/assets/276497b6f3ed88f51e3b08eef008ac5e2340748b73628d187d90c750ca68684e.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $MON$ with perimeter $P=1$ units. What is the largest possible area $A$ of the circular sectors?",
      "worked_solution": "First,  let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $1$ unit, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n1=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{1-2r}{r}=\\frac{1}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{1}{r}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=\\frac{1}{2}-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{1}{4})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{1}{4})$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nTherefore, the circular sector of the largest area will have a radius of $\\frac{1}{4}$, and the area is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4})-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
