    "q-315935": {
      "dom_id": "step-q315935",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14274523/assets/bdaa654a3023641a388526ccf90d4afdc3bae4dd3a88a218a481a90749cb2625.png)\n\nDetermine the radius $r$ of the circular sector with the largest possible area that can be made with a wire that's $40\\,\\text{cm}$ in length. Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}$",
      "worked_solution": "First,  let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $40\\,\\text{cm},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n40=2r+\\theta r.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{40-2r}{r}=\\frac{40}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{40}{r}-2) \\\\  & =20r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=20-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 20-2r & =0 \\\\ -2r & =-20 \\\\ r & =\\frac{(-20)}{(-2)} \\\\ r & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=10$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(10)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(10)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=10$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nTherefore, the circular sector with the largest area will be that in which the radius $r$ is equal to $10\\,\\text{cm}.$",
