    "q-345019": {
      "dom_id": "step-q345019",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14274523/assets/41873a669395a87c248ef7bb837263cbfb669be8d6043a8d88e586adcc3fe76d.png)\n\nA contractor wants to design an entrance sign made up of a rectangle topped by a semicircle, as shown in the diagram. The area of the sign must be $100\\,{cm}^{2}.$ What should be the radius $r$ of the semicircle so that the perimeter of the sign is minimized? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}$",
      "worked_solution": "We let $r$ be the radius of the semicircle and let $x$ be the height of the rectangle. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2x+2r+\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $100\\text{ cm},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n100=2rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $2x$ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =2rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2} \\\\ 2rx & =100-\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{100}{r}-\\frac{\\pi r}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{100}{r}-\\frac{\\pi r}{2}+2r+\\pi r \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{r}+\\frac{\\pi r}{2}+2r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{100}{{r}^{2}}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{100}{{r}^{2}}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{100}{{r}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\pi +4}{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +4} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{200}{\\pi +4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\sqrt{\\frac{200}{\\pi +4}}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{200}{{r}^{3}}>0,$ the stationary point \n$r=\\sqrt{\\frac{200}{\\pi +4}}$ is a minimum.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
